      "narrative": "Mara Venn sets down her cup of dark ale, the ceramic clinking against the scarred oak of the table. Her sharp eyes hold yours as she leans forward, charcoal coat rustling.\n\n\"The Ash Gate,\" she says, voice low and dry as old paper. \"It's been sealed since Old Bram turned up dead at the landing. His boat, the *Grey Hind*, still rots there \u2014 no one's touched it. The Kestrel Guild locked the gate proper the same morning they found him.\"\n\nShe pauses, fingers tracing the rim of her cup.\n\n\"Bram was the only one who worked the ferry through that gate. Without him, the passage is just... a cracked black arch in a field. The Guild claims it's for public safety \u2014 that something on the other side might have killed him. But they've been awfully quiet about what they found at the landing.\"\n\nHer gaze flicks toward the tavern's back window, where through the rain-streaked glass you can just make out the dark silhouette of the gate against the grey morning.\n\n\"Toran Vex over there,\" she nods subtly toward the nervous clerk with ink-stained cuffs, \"he maintained the gate's lock for years. Sworn to the Kestrel Guild. Might know more about *why* they sealed it so fast \u2014 but good luck getting him to talk. He's been jumpy as a cat in a kennel since Bram died.\"\n\nShe takes a long drink, then adds, \"That's the shape of it. Gate's shut. Bram's dead. Guild's not talking. And anyone with questions is drinking alone in the Copper Finch.\"\n\nShe raises an eyebrow at you. \"What's your angle, ranger? Looking for work, or looking for trouble?\""
    },
