      "narrative": "The corner of Mara's mouth twitches \u2014 almost a smirk \u2014 as she sets her cup down and folds her arms on the table.\n\n\"Old Bram was the only one who sold passage. Plain and simple. You want to go through the Ash Gate, you paid Bram. He ran the ferry, he held the key to the lock mechanism on this side, and he decided who crossed.\"\n\nShe ticks off fingers. \"No secondary tickets. No agents. No deputies. Just Bram, his boat, and his purse. He'd take your coin, row you across, and come back with or without you depending on the day.\"\n\nShe pauses, scratching at a stain on the table's surface.\n\n\"Now, *who* bought passage from him \u2014 that's a longer list. Traders mostly. A few couriers from the Sable Quill \u2014 that's a messenger guild, operates out of a little booth near the gate. And...\" she hesitates, \"some folk who paid in silver and didn't give names. Bram wasn't the type to ask questions if the coin was good.\"\n\nShe looks up at you, her sharp eyes narrowing just slightly.\n\n\"One name I do remember, though \u2014 the last fare he took before he died. A woman. Dark cloak, no guild mark. She paid him at dusk, and he pushed off into the mist. She came back alone an hour later. Next morning, Bram was dead on the landing.\"\n\nShe lets that sit.\n\n\"Never caught her name. But Toran might've seen her face \u2014 he was locking up the gate mechanism that evening.\""
    },
