      "narrative": "The Copper Finch's door swings shut behind you, cutting off the low murmur of the tavern. The morning air hits \u2014 damp, cold, carrying the smell of wet stone and turned earth.\n\nA narrow lane runs between shuttered buildings, cobblestones slick with rain. At its end, past a sagging wooden fence and a field gone to weeds, the Ash Gate rises.\n\nIt's not what you expected.\n\nNo grand arch. No wrought iron. Just a crack of absolute blackness framed in pitted stone \u2014 two pillars of dark granite, veined with something that glitters faintly even under the grey sky. The space between them is not empty. It's a void. A flat, depthless sheet of darkness that seems to drink the light around it.\n\nThe ferry landing is twenty feet to the left. A rotting wooden dock juts into a narrow channel of sluggish, ash-coloured water. The *Grey Hind* \u2014 Old Bram's boat \u2014 sits half-sunk against the pilings, its hull splintered, its mooring rope frayed and dangling.\n\nAnd the gate itself: welded shut. Fresh scars of blackened metal run across the lock mechanism \u2014 a heavy iron housing bolted to the right pillar. The keyway has been hammered flat. Bolts are fused, the metal still showing the ripple marks of a smith's hammer.\n\nYou're alone out here. The field is quiet. Just the drip of water from the gate's stone lip, and the faint creak of the *Grey Hind* against the dock.\n\nThe void between the pillars watches. Silent.\n\nWhat do you do?"
    },
